<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- Tawara's privacy manifest. release.sh copies it to the root of Tawara.app
     (docs/RELEASE.md section 6.5; docs/DECISIONS.md D36).

     Required-reason APIs, from the imports of the aarch64-apple-ios release binary
     (nm -u, checked 2026-10-18; that build is linked with the iOS 27.0 SDK, the one
     installed on the machine where it was checked, so the imports must be checked
     again on the iOS 26.x build before submission):
     - stat, lstat, fstat and fstatat. The store's folder and files are under
       Library/Application Support/Tawara in the app's own container, and the app
       makes that folder 0700 (platform/ios/README.md). These calls are taken to read
       the metadata of that container: reason C617.1, "access timestamps, size, or
       metadata for files in the app container". The owner's Xcode privacy report on
       the archive confirms which calls they are.
     - mach_absolute_time and mach_timebase_info, which Rust's Instant uses to measure
       the time between events in the app: reason 35F9.1.
     Not imported, so not declared: the disk-space calls (statfs, statvfs, the
     volume-capacity attributes of getattrlist), NSUserDefaults, and the
     active-keyboard API.
     clock_gettime is imported (by the standard library). No reason code for it was
     found in the sources checked, so it is not declared here. The App Store
     validation of the 26.x archive is where that is confirmed.

     NSPrivacyTracking is false: the app does not link data about the person with
     other companies' apps or websites for advertising, and it has no tracking
     domains.

     NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is empty. The reasoning is that the app runs no
     analytics and no crash reporting of its own, and sends the node only the requests
     the person makes to the node they choose (the default is https://api.mochimo.org,
     DECISIONS D43). Who operates the default node, and whether it keeps those
     requests beyond answering them, is open (DECISIONS D43 item 5; the threat
     model's T28). That is for the owner to decide before submission. The App Store
     Connect privacy answers must agree with this file.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
